    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d396">

                <pb facs="0323" n="311" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg311"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00111A.R.–N.C./132</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Legal Adviser</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Hackworth</hi>)<anchor n="77" xml:id="d396-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-05-14">May 14, 1940</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I see no objection to this recommendation on the Security Zone,<anchor n="78"
                        xml:id="d396-f2"/> with the exception of paragraph 12 of the preamble,
                    inasmuch as it will be for the Governments to decide whether they desire to
                    adopt the recommendation.</p>
                <p>Paragraph 12 deals with a very controversial question and does not, in my
                    opinion, dispose of it in a satisfactory manner. It seems to take the position
                    in effect that belligerent merchant ships may be permitted to operate within the
                    zone free from the right of capture on the part of the opposing belligerent. I
                    doubt whether any belligerent would acquiesce in the shielding by neutrals from
                    the recognized right of capture of a large fleet of enemy merchant ships.</p>
                <p>This, in my opinion, was the most difficult point in the series of questions
                    raised by the British Government. As a practical matter, the issue may never
                    arise. Had I been concerned in the preparation of the document, I should have
                    recommended the omission of the entire paragraph. It may now be too late to have
                    it omitted but it is well to bear the matter in mind should the recommendation
                    come before the Governments in its present form.</p>
                <p>I shall leave to Mr. Duggan the preparation of a communication to Mr.
                    Fenwick.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Green H.
                    Hackworth</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
